SGI Octane / IP30: entry, firmware and address design
======================================================

Status: M1 design, derived from the retained IRIS firmware runs and a source
audit at NetBSD 93a258077244ea6e6a1a43a9d2e8b8fa41aa6555 plus the handover
commit.  This is an entry-proof design, not a completed bootstrap.  Every
claim below is either observed in the retained Octane run, stated by the
IRIS IP30 model documentation, or marked as a source-audit task for M2.

Evidence used
-------------

* The retained ARCS64 diagnostic run: SPB at 0xffffffffa0001000, signature
  0x53435241, length 0x90, vector pointer 0xa800000000001800, vector length
  covering service 30, one R10000, 512 MiB and 0x20000 4-KiB pages.
* IRIS docs/octane-ip30-address-map.md: IRIX's IP30 kernel lives in XKPHYS at
  0xa800000020010000 (physical 0x20010000); only the first 512 MiB is reachable
  through KSEG0/KSEG1 and that window holds no Octane RAM.
* IRIS src/dev/heart_mem.rs: physical 0..0x3fff aliases the first 16 KiB of
  SDRAM; physical 0x20000000..0x20003fff is a bus error; RAM is reachable at
  0x20004000 and above.
* NetBSD source audit listed under "Audit results".

Firmware ABI decision
---------------------

ARCS64 is a distinct interface and must not be fed through the existing
32-bit structures or the o32 call bridge.

Observed/required layouts (no host-layout inference):

  arcbios_spb (existing, 32-bit)     ARCS64 SPB (retained Octane)
  -----------------------------      ----------------------------
  uint32 signature                    uint64 signature         @ 0
  uint32 length                       uint64 length            @ 8
  uint16 version                      uint16 version           @ 16
  uint16 revision                     uint16 revision          @ 18
  int32 restart/debug/...             uint64 restart/debug/
                                             exception/utlb    @ 24..55
  uint32 vector length                uint64 vector length     @ 56
  int32 vector                        uint64 *vector           @ 64

  struct arcbios_mem (existing)      ARCS64 memory descriptor
  -----------------------------      -------------------------
  uint32 type                         uint32 type              @ 0
  uint32 base page                    (4 bytes padding)        @ 4
  uint32 page count                   uint64 base_page         @ 8
                                      uint64 page_count        @ 16

Service numbers are shared.  ARCS64 vector entries are 64-bit function
pointers and the calls use the N64 ABI.  The retained run validated
GetMemoryDescriptor (18), Write (27) and GetEnvironmentVariable (30).
EnterInteractiveMode is index 7 in the same vector.

NetBSD today
------------

* sys/dev/arcbios/arcbios.h defines only the 32-bit SPB, component and
  memory descriptor.
* sys/dev/arcbios/arcbios_calls.S fetches vector entries with INT_L and,
  for LP64 sgimips, translates arguments/stack to low KSEG0 aliases and an
  o32 call frame.  That bridge assumes 32-bit firmware vectors and cannot
  represent the Octane RAM or XKPHYS pointers.
* sys/arch/sgimips/sgimips/machdep.c takes a 32-bit argv and jumps directly
  into arcbios_init(), which parses the 32-bit SPB.  It would misread an
  ARCS64 SPB.

M1 uses a dedicated early path: it validates and calls the ARCS64 vector
directly before any existing ARCS32 code runs.  The general ARCS64
component-tree, console and reboot interfaces are M2/M3 work; the existing
O2/Indy path is untouched.

Kernel address model
--------------------

Decision: a full-width direct XKPHYS cached kernel, no -msym32.

  TEXTADDR / ELF p_vaddr   0xa800000020010000
  physical base            0x0000000020010000
  CCA                      5 (cacheable coherent, write-back,
                            exclusive-on-write)

Reasons:

* KSEG0 and KSEG1 cover only physical 0..0x1fffffff.  Octane RAM begins at
  physical 0x20000000, so neither segment can directly map kernel RAM.
* -msym32 tells the compiler and assembler that every symbol fits in a
  sign-extended 32-bit value.  XKPHYS addresses do not fit, so the IP30
  kernel is built with -mabi=64 and no -msym32.  GAS then emits full 64-bit
  absolute sequences for PTR_LA, `la`, and absolute load/store macros.
* NetBSD's LP64 pmap already understands XKPHYS: pmap_md_map_ephemeral_page,
  pmap_md_direct_mapped_vaddr_p, pmap_md_pool_phystov and
  mips_init_msgbuf have XKPHYS cases.
* IRIX's own IP30 kernel uses the same address family.  We start at physical
  0x20010000 rather than IRIX's 0x20004000 so the ELF PT_LOAD segment's
  64-KiB alignment does not extend back over the 0x20000000..0x20003fff
  bus-error guard.
* 0xa800000020010000 encodes CCA 5, not CCA 2.  CCA 2 is uncached; CCA 5 is
  cacheable coherent write-back exclusive-on-write.  M2 must reconcile this
  entry alias with mips_options.mips3_xkphys_cached, which defaults to
  MIPS_PHYS_TO_XKPHYS(3,0) (0x9800...) for this CPU because the R10000 entry
  has no special CCA flag in this tree.  Cache and pmap code must either use
  the aligned CCA or the address model must be changed coherently.

KSEG0/KSEG1 remain valid for the first 512 MiB of physical space.  On the
Octane that is the 16-KiB low alias, HEART's PIU registers, the widget short
windows and the boot flash.  In particular the firmware SPB is reached
through the KSEG1 low alias at 0xffffffffa0001000 (physical 0x1000), exactly
as the retained diagnostic does.

Entry, stack and BSS
--------------------

Kernel ELF entry remains locore.S:`start`, now at an XKPHYS address.  For
XIRI_IP30 only, before the generic entry logic:

1. Clear .bss from __bss_start to _end.
2. Set sp to the top of xiri_ip30_bootstack, a 16-KiB array reserved in .bss.
3. Skip locore.S's PROM-stack heuristic, which would otherwise replace sp
   with start - CALLFRAME_SIZ.

`start` then performs its normal CP0 setup (interrupts off, KX/UX enabled),
records PRID/FPUID, sets curlwp and calls mach_init().  mach_init's first
IP30 action is xiri_ip30_early(), before bootinfo or ARCS32 parsing.

xiri_ip30_early() deliberately ends in a nop/branch spin loop after its success marker.
It does not install exception vectors, enable interrupts, create a pmap,
enter autoconfiguration or claim any device.  It is an entry proof, not a
booted kernel.

Memory and vector reservations (to enforce through M2)
------------------------------------------------------

The retained 512-MiB descriptor order was:

  ExceptionBlock      0x00000000..0x00000fff  (low SDRAM alias)
  SystemParameterBlock 0x00001000..0x00001fff
  FirmwarePermanent   0x00002000..0x00003fff
  FreeMemory          0x20004000..0x2007ffff  (diagnostic run)
  LoadedProgram       diagnostic image
  FreeMemory          rest, with a FirmwareTemporary hole
  FirmwareTemporary   0x20f00000..0x20ffffff in the retained run
  FreeMemory          to 0x3fffffff

Rules for the kernel:

* Reserve the low 16-KiB alias for the exception block, SPB and permanent
  firmware data.  The low exception vectors can still be written through
  KSEG0(0), because that is the same low alias.
* Exclude physical 0x20000000..0x20003fff even though HEART's descriptors
  describe it: CPU accesses there are bus errors on this board.
* Honor descriptor types rather than total size.  LoadedProgram covers the
  kernel image; FirmwareTemporary/Permanent and SystemParameterBlock are not
  free RAM.  BadMemory must stay excluded.
* Fix the KSEG0 assumptions before enabling allocation:
  - mips_page_physload() converts vkernstart/vkernend with
    MIPS_KSEG0_TO_PHYS(), which is wrong for XKPHYS kernel text.
  - Several cache and pmap paths build KSEG0 addresses from physical
    addresses; they are only valid for physical below 512 MiB.  PMAP's
    LP64 XKPHYS cases must be used instead.
  - The .stub tlb_update_addr and exception stubs that use explicit
    lui/%hi/%lo on kernel symbols need PTR_LA-style full-width sequences.
  - arcbios 32-bit calls must never run against the ARCS64 vector.

HEART, vectors and timekeeping are M2 work.  The relevant routing summary
from the IRIS address-map document is retained in the handover: HEART has
64 interrupt vectors in five processor interrupt groups, the timer raises
vector 50, and widget packets target HEART's ISR at widget-8 offset 0x80.

M1 acceptance artifact
----------------------

The boot prints:

  XIRI_IP30_KERNEL_ENTRY
  kernel.text / kernel.entry
  kernel.sp and CP0 Status/PRID
  kernel.bss start/end, a zero BSS witness, and the boot-stack base/top
  arcs64 length/version/revision/vector
  memory descriptor count, total pages and reserved/free-pages summary
  XIRI_IP30_KERNEL_ENTRY_OK

The probe then stops the emulator and records registers, CP0 and memory.
It enforces the checkpoint: kernel.entry must equal the image ELF e_entry,
kernel.sp must lie inside [kernel.stack.base, kernel.stack.top), and the BSS
witness must be zero.  A successful run must also show
emulator_stopped=true and originals_unchanged=true.  The image hash and ELF
headers must identify the NetBSD kernel, not
xiri/platforms/ip30/arcs64-probe.

Audit results and M2 blockers
-----------------------------

* sys/arch/mips/conf/Makefile.mips force-adds -msym32 for all LP64 MIPS
  kernels.  XIRI_IP30 selects MIPS_SYM32=no; the ELF32 wrapper is disabled
  because it cannot represent the kernel address.
* sys/arch/mips/mips/locore.S: generic initial stack heuristic is skipped;
  BSS is cleared only for XIRI_IP30.  Other ports keep their behavior.
* sys/arch/sgimips/sgimips/machdep.c: entry hook runs before ARCS32 use.
  Remaining mach_init code (bootinfo, argv, ARCS memory, makebootdev,
  pmap_bootstrap, lwp0 uarea) is not executed in M1 and is not yet
  ARCS64-correct.
* sys/arch/mips/mips/mips_machdep.c: mips_page_physload assumes KSEG0;
  mips_init_msgbuf already has an LP64 XKPHYS path.  Exception vector setup
  and per-CPU uarea setup still need an address-model audit.
* sys/arch/mips/mips/mipsX_subr.S: explicit lui %hi/%lo kernel references
  truncate XKPHYS addresses in exception/TLB paths.
* The kernel entry alias uses CCA 5 while the LP64 default cached XKPHYS
  alias is CCA 3.  Establish one cached kernel CCA before enabling cache
  maintenance or pmap ephemeral mappings, and test cache operations at the
  chosen alias.
* sys/arch/sgimips/sgimips/mainbus.c: passes MIPS_PHYS_TO_KSEG1(addr) to
  children; that is acceptable only for low (<512 MiB) physical devices,
  not RAM or wide XIO windows.  Bridge/XIO mapping is M3 work.
* sys/dev/arcbios: keep the 32-bit path for Indy/O2; add a separate ARCS64
  implementation (components, environment, console, reboot) when M2/M3
  needs more than the early calls.

Regression obligations
----------------------

Any change to locore.S, Makefile.mips or shared MIPS/pmap code requires
building and booting the existing Indy and O2 configurations in addition
to the XIRI_IP30 checkpoint.  Do not repin the qualified O2 kernel, emulator
or disks.  Use separate build directories and sequential builds (maximum
two jobs).
